<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William IV, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1493">1493</a>–1550</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William IV was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1508 to 1550, ruling jointly with his brother Louis X from 1516. In 1516 the two dukes issued the beer ordinance later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinheitsgebot">Reinheitsgebot</a>. A firm defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, William made Bavaria a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> and invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a>, while opposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> power in the Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-iv-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="William IV, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barthel Beham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beham,_Barthel_-_Herzog_Wilhelm_IV._von_Bayern_-_Alte_Pinakothek_BSTGS_2456.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
